## The hill across the river.

From anywhere on the Aswan corniche you can see it: a great slope of golden sand on the west bank, ruled with long stairways and dotted with dark doorways, a small domed shrine on its summit. That dome gives the hill its name, Qubbet el-Hawa, the dome of the wind. The doorways are tombs, cut more than four thousand years ago for the men who held Egypt's southern frontier.

You cross by boat at a cooler hour, when the hillside softens and the climb is kinder.

## The governors who went south

Elephantine, just across the water, was Egypt's gateway to Africa, and its governors were explorers, traders and soldiers. They wrote their lives on their tomb walls, travel writing more than four thousand years old. Harkhuf describes his expeditions to the distant land of Yam, returning with incense, ebony and ivory. He also copied out, with obvious pride, a letter from the boy-king Pepi II, so excited that a dancer was coming home with the caravan that he begged Harkhuf to guard him day and night.

Elsewhere, painted pillars and niches mark the tombs of later governors, and one inscription tells of a son who travelled south to bring his father's body home. On the Signature day, a licensed Egyptologist can join you to read them, while Aswan spreads out below: the islands, the white sails, the town on the far bank.

> They were the men at the edge of the known world, and they wanted to be remembered for going further.

## A monastery in the sand

Behind the hill, the desert begins, and in it stand the high walls of the Monastery of St Simeon. Founded by Coptic monks over a thousand years ago and fortified like a castle, it was home to a community of monks until it was abandoned in the thirteenth century. Inside the walls are a church, vaulted halls, cells and storerooms, silent now and warm with the colour of the sand around them.

## Left to us

The boat, the tickets, the hour: your concierge takes care of it, with local knowledge and our craft. Water, shade and rests are built in, and the day ends back on the river as the sun drops behind the dunes. Afterwards, supper by the water at a table we know.
